{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792329640,"nonce":"LkkWY/brwdfAxBEH6QzwiA/IilU=","title":"A canvas keeps its tessellation after it is uploaded: 98 MB for a street map","message":"The user's report: the demo uses a gigabyte of tab memory. A census of the banner's first frame (a forced full collection around each canvas) puts 98 MB on one canvas, the street map's: 381 000 vertices as a 21 MB float array, an 18.6 MB buffer of the bytes uploaded, 1486 cached paths.\n\n**What is kept, and why**\n\nA canvas keeps the triangles, each path's copy, the bytes it uploaded and its lists of draws and clips, to build into again: a canvas that redraws every frame reuses them, and a path drawn twice is moved, not tessellated again. A canvas that is not rebuilt draws from the GPU's copy and has no use for them.\n\n**Fix**\n\nTwo seconds after a build the canvas gives them back, `Mesh.release` and the rest. A canvas rebuilt more often than that never does. On the desktop the pointer-free live data went from 126 to 97 MB and the map's 17.6 MB buffer left the census.\n\n**Not fixed**\n\nIn the browser the collector keeps what is given back: the same release frees nothing there, and the live heap stays at 184 MB (Ash 859f6d5). The release is right regardless, and the wasm collector is next.","files":null}]}